index 2cfdf66..bc5d17f 100644
--- a/ARCHITECTURE.md
+++ b/ARCHITECTURE.md
@@ -446,18 +446,19 @@ Key decisions:
   have needed (between about 31° and 50° on common windows; the fit's is 36°). A zoom
   scales the picture evenly and changes nothing else, so every window of a layout sees a
   scaled crop of one picture of the scene: the page shows that picture
-  (`screens/LandingPicture.tsx`, `public/landing/<layout>.avif` and `.webp`, larger than
+  (`screens/LandingPicture.tsx`, `public/landing/<picture>.avif` and `.webp`, larger than
   any common window of the layout and fading into the night at its edges, the fade drawn
-  into the picture) from its first render, placed by the window's lens (`picturePlacement`,
+  into the picture; a phone's cut, `phone-tall`, for the phones it covers, about a third of
+  the pixels: a window takes the first of its layout's pictures that covers it) from its first render, placed by the window's lens (`picturePlacement`,
   plain arithmetic with the fit's numbers baked, from the window's size as the page first
   renders, nothing measured: the start page is the entry), and the built page's head starts
-  the AVIF downloading before the entry has run (`preloadLandingPicture`, on `/` alone; written
+  the AVIF downloading before the entry has run (`preloadLandingPicture`, bundled with the lens, on `/` alone; written
   in after `<meta name="viewport">`, since before it a phone's window reads 980 px wide and the
   script would fetch the tablet's picture: `vite.config.test.ts`). The built page also places
   the picture from an inline script after `#root` (`screens/earlyPicture.ts`; the phone
   layout alone, as a desktop's first click measured slower), so a phone paints it as it
   arrives, before the entry; `LandingPicture` takes that very element in as
-  its own in its first commit (while its layout's file fits the window), and
+  its own in its first commit (while its file is the window's), and
   `earlyPicture.test.tsx` holds the two markups equal.
   Each layout's picture comes at two densities, picked by the screen's (`srcset`): 1x, and
   `@2x` as fine as the preview's canvas draws on a finer screen (the pixel budget's); AVIF
diff --git a/CLAUDE.md b/CLAUDE.md
index c7dfea3..21d5040 100644
--- a/CLAUDE.md
+++ b/CLAUDE.md
@@ -15,7 +15,7 @@ Client (run from `client/`):
 - `node scripts/showcase.mjs --lobby [--side white|black|random] [--level easy|medium|hard] [--reduced] --out <dir>` — records the way into a game against the computer, frame by frame on the virtual clock (the side, the level, the computer's arrival, the handover, the game's entrance), with a timed contact sheet; needs only Vite running. Use it after any change to the lobby's beats, and read the sheet for black or repeated frames and beats that overlap.
 - `node scripts/pieces.mjs [--piece <type>] --out <dir>` — saves the piece gallery (`pieces.html`, dev only) as a PNG: every piece from the side, three-quarters and above, light and dark; needs only Vite running.
 - `node scripts/sculptures.mjs --target <square> [--orbit <deg>] --out <dir>` — the garden's neon sculptures up close through the dev-only viewer (`sculptures.html`): a sheet from several sides, or a turn round one; needs only Vite running.
-- `node scripts/landing-pictures.mjs` — re-renders `public/landing/{phone,tablet,wide}{,@2x}.{avif,webp}`, the start page's pictures of its preview, shown until the live preview fades in over them (ARCHITECTURE.md "Landing page"); starts its own Vite, no backend, ffmpeg encodes. Rerun and commit them after a visible change to the preview's opening (the tower, the pieces, the garden), with the README's picture: an out-of-date picture shows as a change at the handover.
+- `node scripts/landing-pictures.mjs` — re-renders `public/landing/{phone-tall,phone,tablet,wide}{,@2x}.{avif,webp}`, the start page's pictures of its preview, shown until the live preview fades in over them (ARCHITECTURE.md "Landing page"); starts its own Vite, no backend, ffmpeg encodes. Rerun and commit them after a visible change to the preview's opening (the tower, the pieces, the garden), with the README's picture: an out-of-date picture shows as a change at the handover.
 - `node scripts/readme-image.mjs` — re-renders `docs/preview.jpg`, the README's picture (the start page, the pieces on their starting squares before the demo's first move), and `public/og.jpg`, the same frame cut to 1.91:1 for link previews; starts its own Vite, no backend. Rerun and commit both after a visible change to the board or the start page.
 - `npm run bake:pieces` — rewrites `src/three/pieces/knight.medium.ts` and `src/three/scene/occlusion.medium.ts`, the medium knight's meshes and the medium set's occlusion, precomputed; run it after changing any piece's shape or the occlusion bake (`knightData.test.ts` / `occlusionData.test.ts` fail while they are stale). `golden.test.ts` pins the piece set's geometry by hash: a change meant only to be faster must leave it alone.
 - `node bench/run.mjs [--quick] [--primary] [--only client,server,browser] [--compare <dir>] [--repeat N] [--files engine --grep <case>]` (repo root) — the benchmark suite, each tier in turn, into `bench/out/RESULTS.md` (ARCHITECTURE.md "Benchmarks"), led by the primary rows (`bench/primary.mjs`: one per moment a player waits on, from the player's action to what they see). For a perf change use `--primary --base <ref>` (interleaved A/B of the primary rows against a worktree of that commit, about 13 minutes; report in `bench/out/AB.md`; without `--primary`, `--base` runs the tiers the diff can move; narrow with `--only client --files engine --grep <case>`): comparing against a saved run (`--compare`) is fooled when the shared VM's speed drifts between the runs, which A/A tests showed it does by 50–100% over minutes. The browser tier builds and serves the client and starts its own backend. Benchmark runs contend with anything else on the machine: run nothing else meanwhile. If the client tier stops on a fixture check, the engine's rules changed (run `npm run test`); `EXPECTED` in `client/bench/fixtures.ts` is only updated when a fixture changes on purpose. A new player moment, or one measured differently, goes in `bench/primary.mjs` with the row that measures it.
